  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-num">06</span>
      <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Outlook</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="section-divider"></div>

    <p>The 2026 catalog presents a radically transformed orbital environment relative to any prior decade. Five structural findings merit direct policy attention:</p>

    <div style="margin-top:16px;">

      <div class="callout orange" style="margin-bottom:14px;">
        <strong>1. Single-operator concentration risk.</strong> One commercial operator (SpaceX/Starlink) controls 67.78% of all active tracked satellites. Standard spectrum-coordination frameworks, debris-liability regimes, and conjunction-risk models were not designed for this degree of concentration. Any Starlink operational anomaly or policy change affects the majority of the cataloged fleet.
      </div>

      <div class="callout" style="margin-bottom:14px;">
        <strong>2. LEO is effectively full of one constellation.</strong> 86.45% of all active satellites are in LEO, and 83% of all LEO satellites are Starlink. The ~540–570 km altitude band around 53° inclination is the most congested orbital shell in history. Frequency coordination and conjunction screening in this band now requires bespoke treatment.
      </div>

      <div class="callout green" style="margin-bottom:14px;">
        <strong>3. The 2020–2025 launch surge is structural, not cyclical.</strong> 90.5% of the active fleet launched in the last five years. The 2025 cohort (4,329 satellites) alone exceeds the entire pre-2020 fleet. New entrants (Amazon Kuiper, 210 satellites and growing) indicate this trend will not reverse without regulatory intervention.
      </div>

      <div class="callout" style="margin-bottom:14px; border-left-color: var(--accent4); background: rgba(210,168,255,0.07);">
        <strong>4. GEO is stable but marginal.</strong> Only 668 satellites (4.49%) operate at GEO. This traditional broadcast/communications band faces spectrum-crowding pressure from above (Starlink V-band proposals) and below (growing MEO navigation constellations). Its share of the active fleet will continue to shrink as LEO services displace GEO broadband.
      </div>

      <div class="callout orange" style="margin-bottom:14px;">
        <strong>5. Near-circular orbits dominate; HEO nearly extinct.</strong> 98.96% of satellites have eccentricity below 0.01 — reflecting the commercial preference for stable, manageable circular shells. Only 11 objects qualify as HEO under the MEAN_MOTION threshold. This simplifies some debris-modeling assumptions but also concentrates risk in narrow altitude bands.
      </div>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:20px; font-size:14px; color: var(--text-muted);">
      <strong>Policy implications:</strong> Orbital carrying-capacity assessments, spectrum coordination timelines, and debris-liability frameworks must be recalibrated for an environment where a single operator can launch more satellites in one year (2025: 4,329) than existed in total across all operators prior to 2017. The current trajectory points toward a 20,000-satellite active fleet within 24–36 months at observed launch rates — a density that existing international coordination instruments were not designed to govern.
    </p>
